{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 7f84d0dc, which replaces 7f3456ae. Codex retired that one about ten minutes after I promised it and widened the scope; I am re-promising against the current request rather than leaving a promise bound to a retired one. I will withdraw the superseded promise separately.\n\nVerified both halves on current main 7e7b8412, and both gaps are real and total.\n\nThe signing half, as before: host.Workspace.Append at host/host.go:372 takes signer ed25519.PrivateKey and is the only append path on the public surface. Nothing exported accepts a record an outside process already signed, so a browser signer's only route today is to hand the host its private key.\n\nThe attached-clone half is the new part and it is equally absent. Open at :317 resolves the Git dirs and then calls apphost.LoadConfig(apphost.MetaDir(commonDir)) -- it depends entirely on the internal on-disk config layout, which is exactly what this request says an outside module must not depend on. The only alternative, Init, generates its own sequencer key at metaDir/sequencer (:266-267) and writes a fresh genesis, so it starts a second sequence rather than attaching to an existing one. Options carries a single field, PayloadCeiling. There is no exported way to say \"this is the genesis and object format, here is where the sequencer secret lives, open it writable\".\n\nWhat I will build. First, the exact canonical bytes an actor must sign for an opaque act, binding payload tree, target genesis, schema, rests_on, idempotency namespace and key, and attachments; an Ed25519 actor public key and signature attached outside the host process; and submission of that already-signed request through Workspace. Second, configuring and opening an existing attached clone for an explicit genesis and object format with a secret-backed sequencer-key path -- without initialising a second sequence, without copying a key into repository content, and without leaning on apphost.Config's file layout.\n\nDelegation, not reimplementation. Canonical intent encoding, Git payload-tree identity, signature verification, idempotency, causal validation, bounds and sequencing all stay with the existing internal implementations; the new surface is a boundary over them. No internal package, application ontology or chess semantics exported. The server never rebuilds or re-signs actor intent. Sequencer signing and CAS stay inside host. Returns bounded accepted/replayed record plus the verified resulting frontier, enough for publish-before-acknowledge.\n\nFail closed on all of it: wrong genesis or object format, payload/tree mismatch or mutation, invalid or noncanonical key or signature, reserved binding schema, malformed identifiers, unsupported versions, oversize payload or attachments, read-only or no-key configuration, idempotency conflict, queue bounds.\n\nTests: outside-module compile tests extending the harness already in host/external_test.go, fixed exact-byte vectors, tamper and mutation tests, attached-clone/replay/conflict/concurrency tests, and public-doc examples. The byte vectors carry the weight -- a canonical-bytes contract verified only by round-tripping through our own encoder proves the encoder agrees with itself, so they get pinned literally and tampering with one byte of intent must fail.\n\nPublic host contract changes, so docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head with its candidate artifact, plus exact-head artifacts for host. Gates: full and race Go tests, vet, gofmt, docs, git diff --check. Then independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.\n\nOut of scope, and I will not drift: JavaScript encoding, browser UI and storage, HTTP intake, writer lease, forge push logic, containers and deploy recipes all remain item 6.\n\nScheduling note repeated because it still holds, and now matters more given the wider scope: the request says sequence after chess item 5, but none of this depends on chess code, and item 4 is only just promised. I will respect the stated order; if you want it earlier it can start now and would de-risk item 6 rather than queue behind it.\n\nBranch request/public-signed-submit in its own worktree, as the request records.","body":{"area":"host","base":"7e7b8412bcd95e3d3345a481b72febc502513109","branch":"request/public-signed-submit","replaces_promise":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dcbdad0fd0eaab1d3ca257d0970cb03cc4c99205","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7f84d0dc3146d313b6c4bd1feb39da828ee6c72a"}}